// WQL — ARITHMETIC OVER A TYPE THE EL HAS NO TOWER FOR IS REFUSED, AND `u128` /
// `i128` ARE THE ONLY TYPES LEFT IN THAT SET.
//
// ── WHAT THIS FILE USED TO PIN, AND WHY THAT ROW MOVED ──────────────────────
// It pinned `select s.v + 1` over a `u64` column, with the ground "the EL's
// checked arithmetic (`el_add`/`el_sub`/…) computes in i64, and this type's
// values do not survive the normalization into it". Both halves were true and the
// conclusion did not follow: nothing obliged a `u64` column to be normalized into
// i64. The EL has TWO integer towers now — `el_add`/… in i64 and `el_addu`/
// `el_subu`/`el_mulu`/`el_divu`/`el_remu` in u64 — and a node whose operands are
// 64-bit unsigned computes in the second one, taking no cast at all. That half of
// the old refusal is now `tests/logos/pass/wql_arith_u64_tower_e2e`, which
// asserts the ANSWERS at 2^63 and 2^64−1, where the signed reading differs.
//
// ⚠ WHAT DID NOT MOVE IS THIS FILE'S SUBJECT, AND ITS GROUND IS A DIFFERENT ONE.
// `u128`/`i128` are refused not because their values are lossy under some chosen
// normalization but because THERE IS NO 128-BIT CHECKED SET TO COMPUTE IN. Both
// towers are 64 bits wide, so either one drops bits. A refusal about a missing
// tower is repaired by writing the tower; a refusal about a value's survival is
// repaired by not converting — the two have different remedies and this file
// holds the first.
//
// ⚠ REFUSED AT THE OPERAND, WHICH IS WHERE THE CAST IS. The same fact
// (`ElTy.fits`, and past it `el_arith_tower`'s width test) refuses a lossy UDF
// RESULT at its call site (wql_udf_wide_int_ret_fail); this is the other site the
// same `as i64` is emitted at, and the two share the ground text rather than a
// second copy of it.
//
// A `u128` column may still be projected, sorted by, grouped by and compared —
// none of those go through either tower (wql_key_own_type_e2e).
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub id: i64, pub v: u128 }

pub deem shifted(src: &[Rec]) { from src s select s.v + 1 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
